Exercise stimulates the release of endorphins, which are known to improve mood and reduce feelings of depression and anxiety.
Regular physical activity has been linked to lower cortisol levels, which helps reduce the physiological effects of stress.
Numerous clinical studies have demonstrated that individuals who exercise regularly experience significant reductions in symptoms of depression and anxiety.
The brain's reward system is activated during exercise, leading to increased feelings of well-being and motivation.
Exercise promotes neurogenesis, the growth of new neurons in the brain, which can enhance emotional regulation and mental health.